{"title":"KVPY","category":"Engineering","bank":"kvpy","subject":"Mathematics","chapter":"Linear Programming","count":3,"questions":[{"id":51721,"subject":"Mathematics","chapter":"Linear Programming","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"The number of non-negative integer solutions of the equations <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>6</mn><mo> </mo><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn><mo> </mo><mi>y</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>200</mn></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>100</mn></math> 100 is<br />\n ","options":["<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>5</mn></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>7</mn></math>","Infinite"],"answer":2,"solution":"<p>Given equations <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>6</mn><mo> </mo><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn><mo> </mo><mi>y</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>200</mn></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>100</mn></math><br />\nBy Eqs. (i) and (ii), we get<br />\n<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>5</mn><mo> </mo><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn><mo> </mo><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>100</mn><mo> </mo></math><br />\nFor non-negative integer solutions, when</p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mtext> then </mtext><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>30</mn></math></p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mtext> then </mtext><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>25</mn></math></p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn><mtext> then </mtext><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>20</mn></math></p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>11</mn><mo>,</mo><mtext> then </mtext><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>15</mn></math></p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>14</mn><mo>,</mo><mtext> then </mtext><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></math></p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>17</mn><mo>,</mo><mtext> then </mtext><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></math></p>\n\n<p>and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>20</mn><mo>,</mo></math> then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></math> In every case <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>100</mn><mo>-</mo><mo>(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>></mo><mn>0</mn></math> So, total number of non-negative integral solutions are 7 .</p>","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2019 (SA)","_marksId":"614da4899f11b803dd1f9e71"},{"id":51722,"subject":"Mathematics","chapter":"Linear Programming","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"<p>Consider the following two statements<br />\n<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></math>. Any pair of consistent liner equations in two variables must have a unique solution.<br />\n<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>II</mi></math>. There do not exist two consecutive integers, the sum of whose squares is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>365</mn></math> .</p>\n\n<p>Then</p>","options":["both <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>II</mi></math> are true<br />\n ","both <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>II</mi></math> are false<br />\n ","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></math> is true and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>II</mi></math> is false<br />\n ","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></math> is false and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>II</mi></math> is true"],"answer":1,"solution":"<p>(<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></math>) Any pair of consistent linear equation in two variables must have a unique solution. This statement is false. Consistent equation may have unique or infinite solution.</p>\n\n<p>(<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>II</mi></math>) There do not exists two consecutive integers the sum of whose square is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>365</mn></math> . This statement is also false</p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mn>13</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mn>14</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>365</mn></math></p>","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2018 (SA)","_marksId":"614da400449ca9846459fe01"},{"id":51723,"subject":"Mathematics","chapter":"Linear Programming","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"Let $\\mathrm{f}(\\mathrm{x})$ be a polynomial with integer coefficients satisfying $\\mathrm{f}(1)=5$ and $\\mathrm{f}(2)=7$. The smallest possible positive value of $\\mathrm{f}(12)$ is","options":["5","7","27","15"],"answer":2,"solution":"$\\mathrm{f}(\\mathrm{x})=\\mathrm{ax}+\\mathrm{b}5=\\mathrm{a}+\\mathrm{b}7=2 \\mathrm{a}+\\mathrm{b}$\nsolve $\\quad \\mathrm{a}=2\\quad \\mathrm{~b}=3\\quad \\mathrm{f}(\\mathrm{x})=2 \\mathrm{x}+3\\mathrm{f}(12)=24+3\\mathrm{f}(12)=27$","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2017 (19 Nov SB/SX)","_marksId":"6174d213b24695e2a1405dde"}]}